==English==

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|fr|bigot||a sanctimonious person; a religious hypocrite}}, from {{der|en|frm|bigot}}, from {{der|en|fro|bigot}}, of disputed origin. It is most often believed to have derived from the identical Old French derogatory term {{m|fro|bigot}} applied to the overly religious {{w|Normans}}, said to be known for frequently swearing {{der|en|enm|bi God||by God}} (compare {{cog|ang||bī god}}, {{cog|gmh||bī got}}, {{cog|dum||bi gode}}), which is also thought to be the origin of the surname {{m|en|Bigott}}, {{m|en|Bygott}}. (Compare the French use of {{m|frm|goddams|"goddamns"}} to refer to the English in Joan of Arc's time, and {{m|fr||les sommobiches}} (see {{m|en|son of a bitch}}) during World War I). From meaning "someone overly religious" it came to mean "someone overly devoted to their own religious opinion", and then to its current sense.<ref>{{R:Etymonline}}</ref>

The French ''Centre National de Ressources Textuelles et Lexicales'' supports the Germanic origin theory above. Liberman however opines that this has "too strong a taste of a folk etymological guess invented in retrospect" and prefers Grammont et al.'s theory that it derives from {{m|fro|Albigot|t=inhabitant of {{l|en|Albi}}}},<ref>[http://blog.oup.com/2011/10/bigot-2/ OUP blog]</ref> named after the commune in southern France where {{l|en|Catharism}} (also known as Albigensianism<ref>https://www.britannica.com/event/Albigensian-Crusade</ref>) is thought to have originated.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈbɪɡət/}}, {{enPR|bĭgʹət}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-bigot.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪɡət|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# One who is [[narrow-minded]]ly devoted to their own [[idea]]s and [[group]]s, and [[intolerant]] of (people of) [[differing]] ideas, [[race]]s, [[gender]]s, [[religion]]s, [[politics]], etc.
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Aletha Adu|title=Senior Conservatives hit out at Suella Braverman’s ‘racist rhetoric’|work=The Guardian|date=2023-04-13|url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/apr/13/senior-conservatives-hit-out-at-suella-bravermans-racist-rhetoric|issn=0261-3077|author2=Jessica Elgot|author3=Kiran Stacey|passage=A former senior minister from Boris Johnson’s government told the Guardian they believed Braverman was a “real racist '''bigot'''”.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} One who is [[overly]] [[pious]] in matters of [[religion]], often [[hypocritically]] or else [[superstitiously]] so.
#* '''1653''', Urquhart, translating ''Gargantua and Pantagruel'' by François Rabelais, book 1:
#*: He is no '''bigot''' or hypocrite, he is not torn and divided betwixt reality and appearance, no wretch of a rugged and peevish disposition, but honest, jovial, resolute, and a good fellow.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1664|author=Henry More|title=A Modest Enquiry Into the Mystery of Iniquity|page=436
|passage=Thus one part of their Church becomes Sotts and '''Bigots'''; and the other that behold this Scene of things, though they profess themselves of their Church, become a company of profane Atheists and clancular Deriders of all Religion. {{...}} Nay it is a question whether those that do more superstitiously cleave to them, doe it not rather in a kind of confusion and obstupefaction of mind out of fear and suspicion, then any determinate assurance or firm belief of the things they outwardly profess.}}
#* {{RQ:Maturin Melmoth|III||240|Donna Clara was a woman of a cold and grave temper, with all the solemnity of a Spaniard, and all the austerity of a '''bigot'''.}}

